  "worked_solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -{k}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|+(-{k}^{2})\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+(2\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 8)-{k}^{2}(2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =18-2{k}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 18-2{k}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =9 \\\\ k & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-3$ or $k=3.$",
